    {
      "id": "u1",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Definition of the mole and Avogadro's constant",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "En chimie, les atomes, molécules et ions sont **beaucoup trop petits** pour être comptés un par un. On utilise donc une unité de comptage appelée la **mole**.\n\nC’est comme une **douzaine** :\n\n- 1 douzaine = 12 objets ;\n- 1 mole = \\(6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\\) objets.\n\nCe très grand nombre s’appelle la **constante d’Avogadro**, notée \\(N_A\\).\n\n\\[\nN_A = 6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\\ \\text{mol}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nAinsi :\n\n- 1 mol d’atomes de fer contient \\(6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\\) atomes de fer ;\n- 1 mol de molécules d’eau contient \\(6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\\) molécules d’eau ;\n- 1 mol d’ions sodium contient \\(6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\\) ions sodium.\n\nIl faut toujours préciser **ce que l’on compte** : atomes, molécules, ions, électrons, etc.",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "everyday",
        "evidence": [
          {
            "excerpt_index": 0,
            "quote": "C’est comme une **douzaine** :\n\n- 1 douzaine = 12 objets ;"
          }
        ]
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The definition of the mole, the numerical value and unit of Avogadro's constant, and the illustrative counting examples are standard and factually correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u2",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Relationship between particle number and amount of substance",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "La quantité de matière se note généralement \\(n\\) et s’exprime en **moles** (mol).\n\nLe nombre de particules se note \\(N\\).\n\nLa relation est :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N = n \\times N_A}\n\\]\n\nou, pour trouver le nombre de moles :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = \\frac{N}{N_A}}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The mathematical relations between particle count N, amount of substance n, and Avogadro's constant N_A are correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u3",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Worked calculation of the number of water molecules in 2.0 moles",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Combien y a-t-il de molécules dans \\(2{,}0\\) mol d’eau ?\n\n\\[\nN = n \\times N_A\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN = 2{,}0 \\times 6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN = 1{,}20 \\times 10^{24}\n\\]\n\nIl y a donc environ :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1{,}20 \\times 10^{24}\\ \\text{molécules d’eau}}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "abstract_or_hypothetical",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The calculation 2.0 * 6.022 * 10^23 = 1.2044 * 10^24 rounded to 1.20 * 10^24 molecules is mathematically and physically correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u4",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Definition of molar mass",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "La **masse molaire** est la masse d’une mole de substance. Elle se note \\(M\\) et s’exprime en :\n\n\\[\n\\text{g·mol}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nPar exemple :\n\n- masse molaire de l’hydrogène : \\(M(\\text{H}) \\approx 1{,}0\\ \\text{g·mol}^{-1}\\)\n- masse molaire de l’oxygène : \\(M(\\text{O}) \\approx 16{,}0\\ \\text{g·mol}^{-1}\\)\n- masse molaire de l’eau : \\(M(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}) = 18{,}0\\ \\text{g·mol}^{-1}\\)\n\nCela signifie que :\n\n\\[\n1\\ \\text{mol d’eau} = 18{,}0\\ \\text{g d’eau}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The definition, unit, and example molar masses are accurate.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u5",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Worked calculation of the molar mass of water",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Pour l’eau, \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) :\n\n- il y a 2 atomes d’hydrogène ;\n- il y a 1 atome d’oxygène.\n\n\\[\nM(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}) = 2 \\times M(\\text{H}) + M(\\text{O})\n\\]\n\n\\[\nM(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}) = 2 \\times 1{,}0 + 16{,}0 = 18{,}0\\ \\text{g·mol}^{-1}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "abstract_or_hypothetical",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The calculation summing atomic molar masses to find the molecular molar mass of water is correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u6",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Relationship between mass, molar mass, and amount of substance",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "La relation principale est :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = \\frac{m}{M}}\n\\]\n\navec :\n\n- \\(n\\) : quantité de matière, en mol ;\n- \\(m\\) : masse, en g ;\n- \\(M\\) : masse molaire, en g·mol\\(^{-1}\\).\n\nOn peut aussi écrire :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{m = n \\times M}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The formulas linking mass, molar mass, and amount of substance are stated correctly with standard units.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u7",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Worked example calculating amount of substance and molecule count from mass of water",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Quelle quantité de matière représente \\(36{,}0\\) g d’eau ?\n\nOn sait que :\n\n\\[\nM(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}) = 18{,}0\\ \\text{g·mol}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nDonc :\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{m}{M} = \\frac{36{,}0}{18{,}0}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = 2{,}0\\ \\text{mol}}\n\\]\n\nAinsi, 36 g d’eau correspondent à 2 mol d’eau.\n\nLe nombre de molécules correspondant est :\n\n\\[\nN = n \\times N_A\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN = 2{,}0 \\times 6{,}022 \\times 10^{23}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{N \\approx 1{,}20 \\times 10^{24}\\ \\text{molécules}}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "abstract_or_hypothetical",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The steps computing n from mass and molar mass, and then calculating N using Avogadro's constant, are executed correctly.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u8",
      "kind": "CONCEPT",
      "label": "Relationship between molar concentration, amount of substance, and volume",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Pour une solution, on utilise souvent la **concentration molaire**, notée \\(C\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{C = \\frac{n}{V}}\n\\]\n\navec :\n\n- \\(C\\) en mol·L\\(^{-1}\\) ;\n- \\(n\\) en mol ;\n- \\(V\\) en L.\n\nDonc :\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = C \\times V}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "depth": "explanation"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The formula and units for molar concentration are correctly presented.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u9",
      "kind": "EXAMPLE",
      "label": "Worked example calculating amount of substance in a sodium chloride solution",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "On possède \\(0{,}500\\) L d’une solution de chlorure de sodium de concentration :\n\n\\[\nC = 0{,}20\\ \\text{mol·L}^{-1}\n\\]\n\nLa quantité de matière de chlorure de sodium est :\n\n\\[\nn = C \\times V\n\\]\n\n\\[\nn = 0{,}20 \\times 0{,}500\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{n = 0{,}100\\ \\text{mol}}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "context": "abstract_or_hypothetical",
        "treatment": "worked"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The calculation 0.20 * 0.500 = 0.100 mol is correct.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u10",
      "kind": "CAVEAT",
      "label": "Distinction between moles of molecules and moles of constituent atoms",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "Une mole de molécules peut contenir plusieurs moles d’atomes.\n\nPar exemple, une molécule d’eau est \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\) :\n\n- 1 mol de molécules d’eau contient 2 mol d’atomes d’hydrogène ;\n- 1 mol de molécules d’eau contient 1 mol d’atomes d’oxygène.\n\nDonc :\n\n\\[\n1\\ \\text{mol de H}_2\\text{O} \\Rightarrow 2\\ \\text{mol de H} + 1\\ \\text{mol de O}\n\\]\n\nDe même, pour le dioxyde de carbone \\(\\text{CO}_2\\) :\n\n\\[\n1\\ \\text{mol de CO}_2 \\Rightarrow 1\\ \\text{mol de C} + 2\\ \\text{mol de O}\n\\]",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "subtype": "misconception"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
        "evidence": []
      },
      "accuracy": {
        "verdict": "accurate",
        "reason": "The stoichiometry relating the moles of a compound to the moles of its constituent atoms is correctly explained for H2O and CO2.",
        "errors": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "u11",
      "kind": "STUDY_SUPPORT",
      "label": "Summary table of formulas, quantities, units, and main takeaway",
      "excerpts": [
        {
          "text": "| Grandeur | Symbole | Unité | Formule |\n|---|---:|---:|---|\n| Quantité de matière | \\(n\\) | mol | \\(n = \\frac{m}{M}\\) |\n| Masse | \\(m\\) | g | \\(m = nM\\) |\n| Masse molaire | \\(M\\) | g·mol\\(^{-1}\\) | calculée avec le tableau périodique |\n| Nombre de particules | \\(N\\) | sans unité | \\(N = nN_A\\) |\n| Concentration molaire | \\(C\\) | mol·L\\(^{-1}\\) | \\(C = \\frac{n}{V}\\) |\n\nL’idée essentielle est donc :\n\n> La mole permet de passer du monde visible et mesurable (grammes, litres) au monde microscopique (atomes, molécules, ions).",
          "occurrence": 0
        }
      ],
      "attributes": {
        "subtype": "recap"
      },
      "contextualization": {
        "value": "none",
